WEBVTT

NOTE
The film carries its own burnt-in captions, so this track describes what is on
screen rather than repeating the overlay word for word. It is offered, not
switched on by default: a viewer who can see the picture already has the text.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
[music begins] The title card. One Rust core, four native frontends:
GNOME, Android, a terminal, an MCP server for agents.

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.200
Reprise on GNOME, the whole application, the audio visualizer running.
"One player. Everything you listen to."

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.000
Typing into the library search. Results narrow with every keystroke.
"Instant search — every field, as you type"

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.200
The lyrics pane, scrolling in time with the track.
"Lyrics, in time"

5
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.200
The releases page, filled from the artists in the library.
"New releases — from the artists you keep"

6
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.800
Upcoming concerts, matched to the same artists.
"Concerts nearby — for the same artists"

7
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.000
Subscribed podcasts, their episodes and the position each one was left at.
"Podcasts — shows, episodes, where you stopped"

8
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.200
Library Doctor listing the tags and files it found broken.
"Library Doctor — finds what is broken"

9
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.200
The listening statistics page.
"Your listening, counted"

10
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.600
The library, untouched, blurred behind the request.
"Build me a playlist like Lorna Shore." — asked of an agent, over Reprise MCP

11
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.000
A new playlist has appeared in the sidebar and is showing its hundred tracks.
Nobody touched the window.
"The agent wrote it — MCP, straight into the library"

12
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.400
The frame moves for the only time in the film: the desktop hands over to
the phone.

13
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.600
The same library on Android, searched from the handset.
"Reprise on Android — search, grouped by albums and artists"

14
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.200
An artist page on the phone.
"Straight to the artist"

15
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.800
The newest album starts playing.
"Play the newest album"

16
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.400
The visualizer on the phone — the same engine as on the desktop, rendered
by Android rather than rewritten for it.
"The same visuals"

17
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.000
The end card. The mark lands with the same moves it made on the title card.
